  "answer_text": "Great question! Here's a detailed explanation of why nitrogen is used in plastic welding:\n\n## Why Nitrogen Is Used in Plastic Welding\n\n### 1. \ud83d\udee1\ufe0f It Acts as a Shielding Gas\nNitrogen gas is used when plastic welding because it acts as a shielding gas, protecting the weld area from contaminants in the air. This is much like the shielding gases used when welding steel or aluminum with a MIG welder, to prevent the plastic from oxidizing during the welding process.\n\n### 2. \ud83d\udd25 It Prevents Oxidation and Burning\nAir is composed of 80% nitrogen and 20% oxygen, and the oxygen in the air causes oxidation, burning the plastic during the welding process. This results in smoke, ash, and sparks, which weaken the weld and create contamination.\n\nWelding with nitrogen eliminates this issue by flushing out the oxygen from the weld zone. Without oxygen, the plastic melts cleanly without burning or oxidizing, allowing the melted plastics to fuse together with no contamination for a stronger, more durable weld.\n\n### 3. \ud83d\udcaa It Produces a Stronger, Cleaner Weld\nA nitrogen hot air welder uses compressed nitrogen gas to eliminate oxygen from the weld area. The nitrogen acts as a shielding gas and allows for a contaminant-free weld with less smoke, which creates a stronger weld.\n\nWhen you weld with regular hot air, the oxygen causes oxidation, which weakens the weld and makes it more likely to crack down the road. Nitrogen eliminates this problem entirely.\n\n### 4. \u2744\ufe0f It Helps Control Cooling\nNitrogen also has thermodynamic properties that make it useful when welding plastics like ABS and polypropylene; its high heat capacity allows for faster cooling of the welded material without causing warping or cracking due to thermal shock.\n\n### 5. \ud83d\ude97 It's Ideal for Modern Automotive Plastics\nThermoplastics, such as polypropylene/thermoplastic polyolefin (PP/TPO), which is used to make most bumper covers today, work very well with this type of welder. The nitrogen shielding gas prevents oxidation, ensuring the strongest welds possible \u2014 solid, long-lasting welds that stand up to vibration, cold, and heat.\n\n---\n\n**In summary**, nitrogen is used in plastic welding primarily because it is an **inert gas** that displaces oxygen, preventing the plastic from burning, oxidizing, or becoming contaminated \u2014 resulting in a far stronger and cleaner weld than traditional hot air welding can achieve.",
